", "Cloud holds a roughly 7-to-1 fundraising edge over Tanya Lloyd", "Markets give Lloyd about a 6 percent upset chance; a pro-Democratic national environment is the main offset"], "rationale": "Cloud pairs a ten-point Republican district with a seven-to-one money advantage, and every major rater rates the seat Safe or Solid Republican while markets price a Lloyd upset near six percent. A friendlier Democratic national climate keeps this short of safe, but nothing in the record suggests a genuine contest.", "watch": "A strong Democratic wave or late outside spending for Lloyd could narrow the margin, though the absence of any public polling means little would signal such a shift in advance.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Cloud (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Michael Cloud seeks another term in a South Texas district that leans Republican by about ten points. He enters the general election with a large financial edge, having raised roughly $1.06 million to Tanya Lloyd's $140,000, and with every major race rater calling the seat Safe or Solid Republican. Betting markets give Lloyd only about a six percent chance of an upset.\n\nThe main force working against Cloud is the national climate, which looks more favorable to Democrats than in recent cycles, and mid-decade redistricting has redrawn the district's boundaries. Even so, Lloyd has raised little money, drawn no outside spending, and no public polling of the race has surfaced. Cloud remains a comfortable double-digit favorite, with the seat widely expected to stay in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0435, "margin": -11.3}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0331, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0348, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0491, "margin": -12.5}]}